WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:05.220
The ending of The Invisible Man doesn't unfold
exactly how you expect, but that's only fitting

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.950
for Leigh Whannell's twisty horror-thriller.
The Invisible Man's ending isn't a straightforward

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:14.090
one, but it's the right one for the movie.
Here's how it goes down. Spoilers ahead!

00:00:15.131 --> 00:00:19.949
The Invisible Man's final showdown seems simple.
Cecilia visits Adrian's house for a meal.

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:24.859
When Adrian refuses to apologize, Cecilia
leaves to freshen up. While she's gone, Adrian

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.460
lifts a knife seemingly against his will,
even though he's alone in the room and slits

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.680
his own throat. Cecilia returns just in time
to watch him die.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.180
Everything Cecilia does in this scene is set
up earlier in the movie. The security cameras

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.890
that Adrian used to spy on Cecilia are now
the key to proving his "suicide," and throughout

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.140
the film Adrian's weapon of choice is a knife.
It's only fitting that his story ends with

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:46.700
one.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.640
And then, of course, there's the second invisibility
suit that Cecilia dons, the one she found

00:00:51.649 --> 00:00:56.730
while snooping around Adrian's mansion. That's
why Adrian's comeuppance is so satisfying:

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:01.039
In the final scene, Cecilia is using all of
Adrian's tricks against him. She even sends

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.080
Adrian to the grave with his own catchphrase.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.780
"Surprise"

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.190
During the meal, Adrian pleads for forgiveness,
saying that he's sorry. Is he, though? Because

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:14.250
everything that Adrian does in this scene
screams that he hasn't changed at all. He's

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.830
still choosing Cecilia's meals for her, and
still trying to gaslight her. He blames Cecilia

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:23.040
for her own trauma, deflecting responsibility
and painting himself as the real victim.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:27.340
This is all textbook behavior for an abuser,
and it's not any different than what Adrian

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.700
was doing while he was in the invisibility
suit. From all indications, Adrian hasn't

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.780
learned a thing. Thankfully, Cecilia doesn't
fall for it. She's moved beyond Adrian, and

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.800
she's no longer looking back.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.740
The Invisible Man is framed like an empowerment
story. At the beginning of the movie, Cecilia

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.170
lives in fear of Adrian. Even after she escapes,
she's so traumatized that she can't even leave

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:56.890
the house. Adrian's death gives her solace,
but it's only once the Invisible Man begins

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.701
attacking that Cecilia finally musters the
courage, resolve, and confidence that she

00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:05.620
needs to fight back. By the end of the movie,
Cecilia and Adrian have switched places. Cecilia

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.140
is now the hunter. Adrian is her prey.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:13.600
That's one way to look at it, anyway. Another
is that, despite losing his life, Adrian won.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.980
The Cecilia we met near the beginning of the
film the kind, compassionate woman is gone.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.860
Her sister is dead. She has blood on her hands.
Her emotions have been replaced by a calm,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.000
cool detachedness.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.480
It goes even further than that, though. As
we've seen, Cecilia has learned how to use

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.819
all of Adrian's tricks, including how to get
away with murder. That's not to say that she's

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:38.440
the same as Adrian that dude is still much,
much worse but Adrian obviously broke Cecilia,

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.670
and it's not clear if she'll ever truly recover.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.180
As a thriller, The Invisible Man is taut and
well-paced, but as a result it doesn't always

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:51.210
take the time to explain everything. Most
notably, Cecilia's big $5 million payout a

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:55.970
key piece of Adrian's plan to torment Cecilia,
and one of the key drivers of the plot isn't

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:00.720
mentioned in the last bit of the movie. Presumably,
Adrian's will still applies the second time

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.700
he dies, but we'll never know for sure if
Sydney ends up with enough cash to go to her

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.660
dream college.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.800
Same goes for how Adrian faked his death the
first time presumably, the deception was convincing

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.760
enough to fool the police. Cecilia's revenge
doesn't quite hold up if you think about it,

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.610
either. The authorities know that Cecilia
and Adrian have been mixed up with an invisibility

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:24.210
suit, which was used to commit a violent crime.
Even with the seemingly definitive camera

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:28.670
footage and James' testimony, there's going
to be a pretty thorough investigation, and

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.220
Cecilia is sure to be a prime suspect.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.770
Maybe that's why Cecilia takes off after making
the 911 call. Still, while none of these loose

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:39.870
threads really matter as far as The Invisible
Man's main story are concerned, they're proof

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.990
that the movie's tidy ending isn't quite as
slick as it seems.

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:48.360
Leigh Whannell's The Invisible Man doesn't
need a sequel. By the end, Cecilia and Adrian's

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.430
stories are complete. But this is Hollywood,
and if The Invisible Man is a hit, it's hard

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:55.980
to imagine that Sony and Blumhouse won't try
to build a franchise out of it.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.120
"This movie comes along catering for people
who are just walking in there for the first

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.160
time. And the horror fans are like, 'I know
all this stuff.'"

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:06.400
Clearly, Whannell knows how it goes. In an
interview we had with Whannell, he told us

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.750
that the only Invisible Man material he consumed
before writing the 2020 reboot was H. G. Wells'

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:17.259
original novel and James Whale's 1933 feature
film. He basically ignored all the sequels,

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.200
which is probably for the best.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.580
"Ah-choo!" "Gesundhe--who's there?"

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.440
None of those sequels really follow the plot
established in 2020's The Invisible Man, but

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:33.449
the idea of someone else getting their hands
on the invisibility technology and embarking

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:37.599
on a whole new adventure could be the way
forward. Definitely don't expect a straight-up

00:04:37.599 --> 00:04:41.380
remake of The Invisible Woman, even though
that seems like the most obvious direction

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:47.020
to go. The 1940 feature is actually a screwball
comedy in which a former model uses invisibility

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:52.199
to stop some idiot gangsters. A suitable follow-up
to a moody, paranoid thriller it certainly

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:52.700
ain't.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.940
Of course, there's already an Invisible Woman
feature film in development at Universal,

00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:02.280
but don't be fooled. This isn't a second act
for Elisabeth Moss' Cecilia Kass. It's an

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.039
entirely different project.

00:05:04.039 --> 00:05:08.050
The project comes from Elizabeth Banks, who's
set to direct and star in a more traditional

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.629
Invisible Woman remake for Universal. The
Hollywood Reporter says the movie is like,

00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:17.620
quote, "Thelma &amp; Louise meets American Psycho."
Banks' film is another attempt by Universal

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:22.469
to revitalize its classic horror properties
after Tom Cruise's The Mummy sunk the studio's

00:05:22.469 --> 00:05:26.770
planned Dark Universe franchise right out
of the gate. It's not a follow-up to Whannell's

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:30.870
movie but considering how good The Invisible
Man turned out to be with its smart, sparse

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.300
approach to horror, hopefully it follows his
lead.

